Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: tegra30: surface-rt: enable HDMI via GPIO DD4

HDMI never came up under Linux: the connector's HPD pin (PN7) stayed low and
reads on the DDC bus returned 128 zero bytes, with no NACK. Windows RT drives
PDD4 (GPIO 236) high whenever HDMI works. Comparing full GPIO captures taken
under Windows RT and Linux with the same monitor, PDD4 was one of several
pins Windows holds high that Linux leaves unconfigured. Driving each of
those pins high in turn under Linux, only PDD4 changed anything: PN7 went
high, the EDID read returned a valid header and data, DRM reported the
connector as connected with its 1080p modes, and GNOME extended onto the
monitor at 1920x1080@60. Unplugging and replugging the cable then toggled
PN7 and the connector state as expected.

Describe PDD4 as a fixed regulator and give it to the HDMI connector as
`hdmi-pwr-supply`, which display-connector enables when it probes, before
any hotplug detection. Configure the pad as an untristated output with input
disabled, which is the pad state observed while it worked.

What PDD4 physically switches (connector +5 V, a level shifter, or
something else) has not been measured, so no voltage or input supply is
described. This does not touch the hdmi node's own TODO supplies.

---
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/nvidia/tegra30-microsoft-surface-rt.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/nvidia/tegra30-microsoft-surface-rt.dts
@@ -94,6 +94,15 @@
 				nvidia,enable-input = <TEGRA_PIN_ENABLE>;
 			};
 
+			/* HDMI power enable (PDD4): output, so the pad must not be tristated */
+			hdmi-power {
+				nvidia,pins = "pex_l1_prsnt_n_pdd4";
+				nvidia,function = "pcie";
+				nvidia,pull = <TEGRA_PIN_PULL_NONE>;
+				nvidia,tristate = <TEGRA_PIN_DISABLE>;
+				nvidia,enable-input = <TEGRA_PIN_DISABLE>;
+			};
+
 			vol-keys {
 				nvidia,pins = "kb_row14_ps6", //down
 					      "kb_row15_ps7"; //up
@@ -606,6 +615,7 @@
 
 		hpd-gpios = <&gpio TEGRA_GPIO(N, 7) GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
 		ddc-i2c-bus = <&hdmi_ddc>;
+		hdmi-pwr-supply = <&hdmi_pwr_reg>;
 
 		port {
 			connector_in: endpoint {
@@ -831,6 +841,19 @@
 		vin-supply = <&vdd_5v0_sys>;
 	};
 
+	/*
+	 * Windows RT drives PDD4 high whenever HDMI works. With it low, HPD stays
+	 * low and the DDC bus returns all-zero reads. What PDD4 physically
+	 * switches (connector +5 V, a level shifter) is not known, so no voltage
+	 * or input supply is described.
+	 */
+	hdmi_pwr_reg: regulator-hdmi {
+		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+		regulator-name = "hdmi_pwr";
+		gpio = <&gpio TEGRA_GPIO(DD, 4) GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		enable-active-high;
+	};
+
 	vdd_pnl: regulator-panel {
 		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
 		regulator-name = "vdd_panel";
